- The baseline test is _United Food & Commercial Workers Union v. Zuckerberg_, 262 A.3d 1034 (Del. 2021) --- three prongs, applied director by director, the third asking whether a director lacks independence from someone who benefited. That is the prong § 144(d)(2) presumes away.

- Net: the statute did more work at the pleading stage than its text alone suggested. If a plaintiff cannot get past demand futility, the substantive standard of review never comes up.

=== Questions to hold onto as we move to the competitors.

- Before we cross the state line, four questions. I want answers to at least two of them.

- First: did SB 21 change outcomes, or only change who bears the cost of finding out?

  - [Discussion map. "Only cost" reading: entire fairness survives in § 144(a)(3), (b)(3) and (c)(2), so a genuinely unfair deal can still be attacked --- what changed is that the plaintiff must now plead his way in without emails. "Changed outcomes" reading: _Ayers_ shows dismissal at the pleading stage, and a case dismissed is an outcome.]

- Second: entire fairness survives in form. Does it survive in practice if almost every deal can be papered into a safe harbor?

  - [Point back to the missing _ab initio_ requirement --- that is the papering mechanism.]

- Third, on fees. SB 21 did not cap plaintiffs' attorney fees. Senate Concurrent Resolution 17 asked the Bar to study them, including a cap keyed to a multiple of lodestar, and it was laid on the table on February 17, 2025 --- the day it was introduced.

- So the fee question was not deferred. It was dropped. Was fee exposure the real grievance?

  - [Discussion map. Evidence for: the _Tornetta_ fee arc --- \$5.6 billion sought, \$345 million awarded, \$54.5 million on quantum meruit with a four-times multiplier on reversal. The literature is live: Grundfest argues Chancery fee awards substantially exceed ordinary hourly rates; Klausner responds that the work relies on minimal data and extreme outliers like _Tornetta_. Evidence against: if fees were the grievance, capping fees was available and cheap, and the legislature declined it.]

- Fourth: if the point was predictability, has Delaware's comparative advantage --- a court that decides hard facts well --- just been converted into a liability?

  - [Talley's framing is the sharp version: the amendments "amount to a direct rebuke of the Delaware judiciary," imposing "a legislative clampdown on the very judicial discretion that, for decades, has defined Delaware's distinctive brand." CLS Blue Sky Blog, Feb. 18, 2025.]

